course: dictionary
lesson: 14222
title: Mutually: shared by both
level: primary-6
stars: 2
explain: Mutually means that something goes both ways. It is felt or done by each of two or more people towards the other, or it is shared by both. «Sam and Priya are mutually helpful: Sam helps Priya with maths, and Priya helps Sam with spelling.» Each one helps the other, so the help goes both ways.
explain: Look for clues that something happens between people, not just to one person. «The two teams made a mutually agreed time to play on Saturday.» Both teams said yes to the same time. To check your meaning, swap in "shared by both" and see if the sentence still makes sense.
explain: In maths, mutually has a special meaning. «Mutually exclusive» events cannot both happen at the same time. «When you flip one coin, heads and tails are mutually exclusive.» You get one or the other, never both. Here mutually does not mean friendly or kind; it means the two outcomes cannot happen together.
fact: Mutually means each one does something to, or feels something towards, the other.
fact: «Mutually» means shared by both sides.
fact: «Mutually beneficial» means good for both sides.
fact: «Mutually respectful» means each person shows respect to the other.
fact: In maths, «mutually exclusive» means two things cannot both happen at the same time.
exercise: «The two friends made a mutually agreed plan for the day.» What does mutually mean here? | Chosen by only one friend | *Shared by both friends | Loud and fast | Written in pencil
exercise: «Heads and tails are mutually exclusive when you flip one coin.» What does mutually exclusive mean? | Happen only on Mondays | Are exactly the same thing | Must always happen together | *Cannot both happen at the same time
exercise: «The two neighbours are mutually respectful.» What does this tell you? | They are both very tired | *Each one shows respect to the other | Neither one cares about the other | Only one of them is respectful
test: «Our club's rules were mutually beneficial, so the members and the leader both gained something.» What does mutually beneficial mean? | *Good for both sides | Bad for everyone | Hard to understand | Good for only the leader
test: «Getting an even number and getting a 5 on one dice roll are mutually exclusive.» What does this mean? | They are the same number | *They cannot both happen on the same roll | Both can happen on the same roll | They happen only on odd days
exam: Which sentence uses mutually to mean shared by both? | The dog ran mutually across the field | The sun is mutually hot at noon | *The team and the coach made a mutually agreed plan | I mutually ate my lunch quickly
